<h2>The background</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered in November 2025 at PS Baba Haridas Nagar, Dwarka, alleged that the complainant was sexually assaulted and threatened, with money demanded from her, and that the abuse continued on a false promise of marriage. The chargesheet alleged conduct stretching from 2016 to 2025 and stated that the complainant was thirteen years old at the time of the first incident; the prosecution cited hotel records, bank transfers and medical records, including a terminated pregnancy.</p>
<p>The procedural history matters. Anticipatory bail was dismissed on 9 December 2025; the petitioner was arrested on 15 January 2026; the Fast Track Court dismissed his regular bail application on 6 February 2026; and he had interim medical bail between 30 April and 4 July 2026. By the time the High Court heard the present application, the trial was in prosecution evidence, with the complainant&rsquo;s examination ongoing and the next date fixed for 10 December 2026.</p>

<h2>The stage-of-trial principle</h2>
<p>The court&rsquo;s reasoning tracks a consistent line of Supreme Court authority. From <em>X v. State of Rajasthan</em> (2024), the proposition that a trial under way, particularly during the victim&rsquo;s deposition, is ordinarily to be allowed to run its course:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the judgment, quoting the Supreme Court:</strong> &ldquo;Once the trial commences, it should be allowed to reach to its final conclusion which may either result in the conviction of the accused or acquittal of the accused.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>From <em>Neeru Yadav</em>, the discipline that bail in serious offences is not a matter of routine but of weighed discretion. Set against the gravity of the POCSO allegations and the victim&rsquo;s age, the balance fell against release. The court was careful to record that its observations were prima facie, confined to the bail application.</p>

<h2>What the decision means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Timing is strategy.</strong> A bail application filed while the complainant is mid-deposition faces the strongest headwind the law knows. Where possible, the realistic windows are before evidence begins or after the material witnesses are over.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Changed circumstances remain the key.</strong> Completion of the victim&rsquo;s testimony, demonstrable contradictions, or protracted delay in concluding evidence are the classic foundations for a renewed application. Repetition without change invites summary dismissal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Interim medical bail is not a stepping stone.</strong> A spell of medical bail, duly surrendered, earns compliance credit but does not soften the stage-of-trial objection. The two operate on different axes.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>Bail jurisprudence constantly balances the presumption of innocence against the integrity of the trial process. The stage-of-trial principle is where the second interest speaks loudest: the system&rsquo;s single best protection for a testifying victim — particularly a minor in a POCSO matter — is that the accused&rsquo;s liberty question waits until she has finished her evidence. Delhi&rsquo;s courts apply the principle with regularity, and this decision is a clear, current illustration of it, useful both to defence counsel advising on timing and to complainants&rsquo; counsel resisting mid-deposition release.</p>
